2], [" setup", -1.4384621381759644]]}, {"t": " version", "lp": -0.001245081308297813, "top": [[" version", -0.001245081308297813], ["/kernel", -7.935161113739014]]}, {"t": "?", "lp": -0.00039817867218516767, "top": [["?", -0.00039817867218516767], [" and", -8.812033653259277]]}, {"t": "", "lp": -0.5472664833068848, "top": [["", -0.5472664833068848], [" That", -1.0836739540100098]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Context stealing**: The driver will \"steal\" a CCS from another GPU context when your context needs one but none are available\n- More flexible but can cause **contention** between contexts\n- A CCS is dynamically allocated from the pool when needed\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **No stealing**: The driver will not steal CCS from other contexts\n- If no CCS is available, the operation may **fail** rather than steal\n- More **predictable** behavior, better for workloads where you want to avoid interference\n- Useful when you want explicit control over resource contention\n\n## When to use Mode 4\nMode 4 is typically used when you want to **pin or reserve** CCS contexts, giving you more deterministic behavior. This is relevant when:\n\n- You're running **multiple concurrent workloads** and want to guarantee CCS availability\n- You need **deterministic scheduling** without the overhead or contention of stealing\n- You're doing **performance-sensitive** compute where resource contention matters\n\n---\n\n**A note of caution:** I'm not fully certain about the exact semantics of mode 4 versus the others, and these driver parameters can vary between driver versions and kernel releases. The Xe driver's CCS handling has evolved over time.\n\nWould you like me to help you find the authoritative source\u2014such as the kernel driver documentation or Intel's GPU driver specs\u2014to confirm the precise behavior for your specific driver version?"}